An encounter is a documented interaction between a patient and a healthcare organization or provider for the purpose of receiving or managing healthcare services. An encounter can represent different types of care, such as an outpatient visit, emergency department visit, inpatient stay, telephone interaction, or other clinical interaction, depending on how the healthcare system defines and records it. In EHR data, an encounter usually has information such as an encounter ID, patient ID, provider, encounter type, location, start and end dates, and status. Encounters are important in healthcare analytics because they provide a way to connect clinical activities, diagnoses, procedures, orders, medications, and observations to a specific episode of care. The exact definition and types of encounters can vary between EHR systems and organizations, so analysts should always review the source system's definitions before using encounter data.

In simple terms, an encounter is basically a record of a patient's interaction with the healthcare system. If a patient goes to a clinic for a checkup, that interaction can be recorded as an encounter. If the same patient later goes to the emergency department, that can be another encounter. An encounter does not necessarily mean the patient was admitted to the hospital, because many encounters happen in outpatient or emergency settings. For a data analyst, encounters are useful because they help organize clinical activity around a particular interaction with the healthcare system. Instead of looking at a patient's entire history as one large set of records, analysts can use encounters to understand what happened during specific healthcare interactions.
